1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,960
What you're looking at on screen is actually an overview of how much money our agency has spent on ads in the last 30 days.

2
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:09,840
If you can't see that, it's over $2.2 million.

3
00:00:10,820 --> 00:00:18,660
So, when it comes to media buying, I want you to know that I have tried every single possible way to click buttons and

4
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:23,940
make more money for the brands that we work with as well as for the brands that we consult.

5
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:32,620
Whenever it comes to buttons that you can click to actually make you more money, there are very few inside of Facebook as of 2025.

6
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:40,760
The glory days of being able to magically make more money by clicking one button on Facebook unfortunately aren't here.

7
00:00:40,940 --> 00:00:49,240
But there are still a few that if you click them the right way and you structure them the right way, you can actually turn your ad account into

8
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:54,240
a much more profitable and higher-spending machine for your e-commerce brand.

9
00:00:54,660 --> 00:01:00,020
And in this week's video, I'm going to walk through absolutely everything when it comes to how to structure your ad account,

10
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:05,160
how to properly creative test, how to scale, what attribution settings you need to use,

11
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:12,880
and absolutely everything that I can possibly think of, and literally everything that I know about how to spend millions of dollars

12
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:17,360
for your brand inside of Facebook the best possible way right now.

13
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:23,460
Now, before I move further into this video, I need to make it clear, even if this means half of you click off:

14
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:27,500
There is no ad account structure that will save your bad ads.

15
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:35,060
If your offer sucks, your ads suck, and your landing pages are not good, this structure will not save you.

16
00:01:35,220 --> 00:01:41,460
However, if after implementing everything that I'm going to talk about in this week's video, you still are not able to scale further,

17
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:47,640
then it's your sign that it is not your structure that is the problem. It is those three things that I just mentioned.

18
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:54,580
So, as much as I'm excited for you to see everything about how to structure Meta ads the right way—at least if you ask me—

19
00:01:54,580 --> 00:02:01,560
that if you really want the most out of this structure, you need to put intent and focus into the things that actually matter.

20
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:03,940
Now, with all that said, let's go ahead and jump into the video.

21
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:08,020
Now, first things first: I'm going to be building this in a blank, random ad account.

22
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:14,320
So, again, I do have ad accounts that I will likely pull up to show you the results as we make it through this video,

23
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:19,900
but I'm going to be building this from scratch to help you guys understand how to set these up properly.

24
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:24,920
Now, the thing that I want to clarify right away, and I have to go full screen for this, is that

25
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:32,540
do not turn off all of your campaigns after watching this video to set up your campaigns and do everything that I show you in this video.

26
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:38,380
If you decide to do that and your performance tanks, that is 100% your fault. Okay? Do not turn off—

27
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:44,900
we have one golden rule at the agency, and that rule is if something is working, just leave it on.

28
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:48,860
Okay? There is no point in turning something off that is making you money. So,

29
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:55,900
if you have 100 campaigns or 1,000 campaigns and a whole bunch of crazy stuff all over the place, if it's working, just leave it on.

30
00:02:55,900 --> 00:03:04,160
And what I would encourage you to do is to set these campaigns and follow what I mention in this video as addition to whatever you have going on,

31
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:10,140
because I do not want you to go into your ad account and turn off all your campaigns, tank your ad spend and tank your performance, and say,

32
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:15,480
"Well, this ad strategy didn't work." Well, it's not supposed to replace everything overnight, okay? This is a gradual process

33
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:20,520
to get your account structured the right way, but I just want to make that clear before we actually get into this.

34
00:03:20,520 --> 00:03:25,880
Before we even talk about getting everything set up, the first thing that you actually need to set up is your columns.

35
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:30,660
And you need to understand that your columns are what communicate data to you.

36
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:37,320
So, if you do not have your columns set up and you do not have these custom metrics that I'm about to explain to you, you need to get them set up

37
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:46,160
because they are very, very important for you to actually be able to look at the data in your account and actually get more from the data that Facebook is communicating to you.

38
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:53,740
So, if you want our exact columns, what you're going to do is just take a screenshot of this, or pause the video, and you can see the exact columns that we use:

39
00:03:53,740 --> 00:04:02,560
things like campaign name, ad set name, attribution setting, budget, and we have some custom metrics down here as well like conversion rate, or hook rate, and hold rate.

40
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:10,020
And some of these you guys might be familiar with, but I want you to basically just pause the video, you can see these are the columns that we use.

41
00:04:10,020 --> 00:04:14,740
And again, if you want to find them, for example, you just search them up here, click CPM, and then you can click save.

42
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:24,140
Now, I'm not going to explain what every single one of these mean, but what I will do is show you the custom metrics. So, the custom metrics that we actually make for our brands

43
00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:31,640
are very simple, okay? So, the first one is going to be CVR. So, if we actually look down here, you can see we have CVR.

44
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:40,780
And what CVR is, is very simply purchases divided by landing page views. You could also do purchases divided by link clicks, but if you've never made a custom metric in your life,

45
00:04:40,780 --> 00:04:47,460
you're just going to come in here and search up what it is that I showed you, which the first one is going to be purchases, and then you're going to do divided by,

46
00:04:47,460 --> 00:04:53,620
and then the next one here is again going to be landing page views. And then when you're naming this, again, you could call this Purchase CVR,

47
00:04:53,620 --> 00:05:01,540
and you're going to change this into a percentage, because obviously this is a percentage that you're looking at, and you can make this access to, or you know, basically give access to everybody,

48
00:05:01,540 --> 00:05:08,300
and then you're going to click create metric. Now, I'm not going to do that, cuz I already have the metric, but the other two metrics that we have are video hold.

49
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:15,060
So, video hold is very simple: ThruPlays divided by impressions. And what this tells us is how many people watch

50
00:05:15,060 --> 00:05:20,620
15 seconds of my video ad and so throughplays basically just means 15 seconds

51
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:28,680
and if your ad is shorter than 15 seconds, then you're simply just going to see how many people watch the entire thing. That's what video hold is.

52
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:34,360
And then hook rate is a very common one, this is just three-second video plays / impressions.

53
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:39,300
And so, from there, those are the custom metrics that we set up. We used to create a custom metric

54
00:05:39,300 --> 00:05:46,960
called AOV, but Facebook has since actually come up with their own, it's called average purchase conversion value, and again I will show you where all of these things come

55
00:05:46,960 --> 00:05:51,000
into play. Now from there, another thing that is super important for you to have

56
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:58,700
is the actual indication of what your audiences are, because this is a lot of times when we see media buyers make the mistake,

57
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:03,600
where they will say this is a broad ad set, that is for let's just say women

58
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:09,280
who are 35+, right? And that's what they will call their ad set. Now

59
00:06:09,320 --> 00:06:18,200
if you don't have these columns added, which again if you go into the customized columns here, you can see we have these additional columns here, we can see that we have gender

60
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:25,420
as a indicator, we have audience as an indicator, and we have age as an indicator, and this is super important to have this here,

61
00:06:25,420 --> 00:06:31,120
because this is the data that is actually being pulled from what you have selected. And so, as you can see,

62
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:35,700
my media buyer, or me in this instance, wrote broad ad set women 35+,

63
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:45,100
but we can see, because I have my column set up the right way, we're actually targeting both genders and it's 18 to 65, it's not 35+. So, now we're

64
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:53,900
able to easily catch mistakes, and this is going to be super common when we get into the excluded audiences and the included audiences, because we see so many times

65
00:06:53,900 --> 00:07:00,040
media buyers accidentally include audiences rather than exclude them. So it's very, very important that you have this here

66
00:07:00,040 --> 00:07:04,860
so you can see the data on the ad set level. And then again from the ad side, there's no data here,

67
00:07:04,860 --> 00:07:09,880
but just know that seeing the metrics of conversion rate and average order value really help you understand

68
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:17,060
where your ad is performing well. Same thing with hook rate and hold rate, this just gives you a better idea of how well your ads are hooking

69
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:27,060
in your audience, and how good of a job it's actually doing of keeping them watching that video, and we also like video average play time, because this just gives us a rough estimate of where people are stopping

70
00:07:27,060 --> 00:07:31,960
and where we likely need to fix the video, or just before that, in order to give them a reason

71
00:07:31,960 --> 00:07:37,540
to keep watching. So, that is all of the columns that we have, and again I know we haven't even got into the structure yet,

72
00:07:37,700 --> 00:07:44,980
there's going to be a lot to cover in this video. But again, your data is your truth, so it is very important that you have things structured and set up the right

73
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:49,020
way. Now, the next thing that you need to do, unlike me in this example,

74
00:07:49,220 --> 00:07:55,220
is you need to name your campaigns, you need to name your ad sets, and most importantly you need to name your ads,

75
00:07:55,220 --> 00:08:02,280
because as you saw over here, in my reporting section, what's really cool about this is our agency actually has the

76
00:08:02,280 --> 00:08:09,740
ability to come through here and start to filter specific things by their name. So, if you're not actually putting

77
00:08:09,740 --> 00:08:15,720
time and attention into naming your ads properly, and including variables like this is a video,

78
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:24,600
or this is an iteration, or this is an ideation, or even things as granular like we do based off of editors or based off of, if you're a really big brand,

79
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:30,300
specific agencies or in-house teams that you're working with, and it's so valuable to actually take the time

80
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:36,940
to name your ad sets so again you have clarity and you can be organized in the account. Lucky for you,

81
00:08:36,940 --> 00:08:42,880
if you are watching this video, I will actually attach a naming scheme that we use down below. It's a little bit

82
00:08:42,880 --> 00:08:51,640
complex so you can feel free to simplify it, but if you just want to copy the naming scheme that we have used before, I will give that to you guys and you guys are more than welcome to take that

83
00:08:51,640 --> 00:09:00,080
and install that into your business, because you just need to make sure that you have some kind of naming scheme rather than none at all. You have no idea how many times I do audits on brands

84
00:09:00,080 --> 00:09:05,120
where they just have things like "New sales ad" and "New sales ad copy", and they have no idea what's going on,

85
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:10,860
they can't pull reports properly, and again your data is your truth, so it is so important to make sure

86
00:09:10,860 --> 00:09:17,520
that you know what is going on. So, bare minimum, make sure you name your ads, and ideally you name everything and you stay

87
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:24,300
organized so that way you don't have to click into everything, and again you can always double check by having specific columns set up

88
00:09:24,300 --> 00:09:30,800
like this where you can see your exclusions, inclusions, and all of that kind of stuff. Now this is great and all, but let's talk about the juicy stuff

89
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:35,660
which is actually going to be the structure, okay? The structure that we are going to be setting up is going to be

90
00:09:35,660 --> 00:09:40,440
the 3-2-2-2 structure. Now, you've probably heard of the 3-2-2 before,

91
00:09:40,440 --> 00:09:47,140
which was made famous by Charley T, and he is kind of like the godfather of that, and we have a lot of respect for Charley,

92
00:09:47,140 --> 00:09:55,000
but we've kind of implemented a little bit of our own spice onto this by adding an additional two at the end, which also includes testing specific landing pages.

93
00:09:55,340 --> 00:10:00,020
Now, if you are a brand that is spending less than a thousand dollars per day,

94
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:05,380
you should honestly never have more than one campaign, maybe max two.

95
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:10,820
And the reason for that is because you need all of your budget as consolidated as possible, okay?

96
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:14,120
You need as much spend going into your ads as you can get,

97
00:10:14,120 --> 00:10:20,620
because as soon as you start to spread your spend super thin, it just makes it extremely hard for Meta to optimize.

98
00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:29,320
So, the only two campaigns you should ever have are the 3-2-2-2 campaign that I'm about to show you how to set up, as well as a potential ASC campaign.

99
00:10:29,320 --> 00:10:33,080
With that said, what we're going to do here is we're going to set up our campaign.

100
00:10:33,460 --> 00:10:37,300
So you're going to create a new campaign, and I'll actually do this one from scratch with you,

101
00:10:37,300 --> 00:10:40,340
and what you're going to do is you're going to, obviously, select Sales.

102
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:44,920
Once you select Sales, you're going to click Continue, and here you're just going to select Manual Sales campaign.

103
00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:48,260
For now, we will come back to the Advantage+ Shopping, we're going to click Continue.

104
00:10:48,560 --> 00:10:53,400
And now, again, you're going to name things properly, and just as a small thing, templates are also awesome.

105
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:55,680
You can create your own templates inside of Meta if you want.

106
00:10:55,700 --> 00:11:00,180
And we're going to call this our Acquisition campaign, and that's a word that I always spell wrong.

107
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:07,800
But we're going to call this Acquisition, where it's going to be a US CBO, kind of alluding there, and this is usually what most of our campaigns look like.

108
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:11,300
And inside of here, again, buying type is just going to be Auction, okay?

109
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:18,400
The campaign objective is Sales, the campaign spending limit is just going to be None. We're not going to run catalog ads, we're going to run Advantage campaign budget.

110
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:21,780
With that said, if you are a brand that is multi-SKU,

111
00:11:22,140 --> 00:11:27,140
we can talk about catalog ads after, there's going to be a little mini Q&A section that I'm going to get to,

112
00:11:27,140 --> 00:11:30,920
for any common questions or most frequently asked questions that we get.

113
00:11:30,920 --> 00:11:33,740
But the most important thing here is we're going to turn on CBO.

114
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:38,480
Now, for those of you that don't like CBO or think ABO is better,

115
00:11:38,480 --> 00:11:47,080
I'm going to try to give you an example that will hopefully hit home and help you shift your perspective on why we usually do everything in CBO rather than ABO.

116
00:11:47,080 --> 00:11:54,960
Number one: for those of you that are super stuck with making sure that your ads get spend, you can actually run minimum budgets inside of CBO campaigns,

117
00:11:55,420 --> 00:11:57,540
so it basically gives you the best of both worlds.

118
00:11:57,540 --> 00:12:05,800
If you have a larger budget of say $5,000 or $10,000 or $100,000 a day, you can set minimum budgets on each and every single creative.

119
00:12:05,800 --> 00:12:12,680
And then get the added benefit of CBO with whatever excess spend that you have, it will just go towards your top ads.

120
00:12:13,100 --> 00:12:20,720
So if you're still ride or die for ABO because you're able to force spend, there's no point. You can just run CBO with minimum budget, which is something that we are going to discuss.

121
00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:28,560
And the second reason, which should hopefully make a lot of sense, is because Meta has invested billions of dollars at this point building an AI algorithm.

122
00:12:28,780 --> 00:12:39,440
And what this algorithm does is it analyzes ads every single day and knows and has a pretty good idea of what ads are going to perform in the auction and what ads are not.

123
00:12:39,700 --> 00:12:46,600
And so this is why they came out with this thing called Advantage+ Budget Optimization or Advantage Campaign Budget.

124
00:12:46,920 --> 00:12:56,320
And what it's doing is it's leveraging its AI and its algorithm to basically analyze your ads and figure out which ones are actually going to perform.

125
00:12:56,660 --> 00:13:01,620
And now I know somebody's like, "Thanks for explaining the obvious." And I know, I just need to make sure that we're on the same page.

126
00:13:01,620 --> 00:13:13,260
But more importantly, you need to understand that this algorithm performs a lot like your favorite TikTok algorithm or the Instagram algorithm where you get served things that you like, right?

127
00:13:13,260 --> 00:13:18,740
Just like how you loaded up a YouTube video, and now you're watching this video because you're interested in e-commerce, right?

128
00:13:18,900 --> 00:13:21,100
YouTube has an algorithm that understands you, all right?

129
00:13:21,360 --> 00:13:30,560
And just like how an organic algorithm will recommend videos to you, for example, have videos that go viral, they go viral because they are good.

130
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:34,400
And the platform is pushing it organically because it is good.

131
00:13:34,660 --> 00:13:39,300
It's the exact same thing inside of Facebook with its ad algorithm.

132
00:13:39,300 --> 00:13:48,020
You don't need to force spend on 99% of your ads because Meta has an algorithm that knows what's going to be good, okay?

133
00:13:48,300 --> 00:13:54,340
So, just like how when you post an organic video, if it completely flops, it was probably just a bad video.

134
00:13:54,520 --> 00:14:01,880
And then on the other side, if you post a video organically and it goes viral and it gets a ton of views, it was probably a pretty good video, right?

135
00:14:02,080 --> 00:14:09,380
Now, for some reason, us marketers think we're smarter than the machine, and we think that because Meta is not spending on our ads—

136
00:14:09,380 --> 00:14:12,480
aka in the organic version they're not going viral—

137
00:14:12,920 --> 00:14:17,260
we think Facebook is wrong, and that we're smarter, and that it needs to spend on that ad.

138
00:14:17,520 --> 00:14:21,980
Meanwhile, every time we do that or majority of the time that we do that, it just actually doesn't perform.

139
00:14:22,380 --> 00:14:31,660
Now, the thing that happens to so many people is the odd time you will actually see an ad start to get some decent performance on ABO when you force budget to it.

140
00:14:31,660 --> 00:14:36,440
But the truth is is it rarely ever scales your account, it rarely ever goes viral,

141
00:14:36,820 --> 00:14:40,380
and again, the example of the organic algorithm, and that's the—

142
00:14:40,380 --> 00:14:43,140
problem. We are leveraging Facebook to be able to scale.

143
00:14:43,440 --> 00:14:52,020
So I don't really care if an ad gets two purchases a day when I have ads that are getting me 200 a day that Meta is spending all of my budget on.

144
00:14:52,220 --> 00:15:07,820
So, hopefully that example, I know it was a little, maybe a little bit hard to follow, but the example of the organic algorithm pushing videos that go viral is the exact same as Meta putting spend on your best videos because it has the same algorithm. It knows what's good, and it's going to push it.

145
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:13,360
And so there's no need to really waste a ton of budget on testing your creatives just because you think they're good.

146
00:15:13,560 --> 00:15:15,840
And I promise that is the end of the CBO/ABO rant.

147
00:15:16,080 --> 00:15:19,960
If you still want to test ABO after this, be my guest. I'm not going to tell you that you're wrong.

148
00:15:20,140 --> 00:15:28,600
I'm just explaining from my side of things and trying to help you understand the actual facts around why Meta developed this and why it's actually encouraged for you to use it.

149
00:15:28,620 --> 00:15:35,700
A lot of times, our egos just get in the way, and that's why we default it to forcing spend, but at the end of it all, hopefully that makes sense.

150
00:15:35,960 --> 00:15:42,940
Now that we got over the big ABO question, we can get into the rest of the setup, which is going to be setting a daily budget.

151
00:15:42,940 --> 00:15:48,040
So, set the daily budget to whatever you are comfortable with. I'm just going to throw this at $200 per day.

152
00:15:48,040 --> 00:15:55,900
Now, this is something that I do want to call out as well, because Meta has actually moved to a weekly budget, similar to how Google functions, rather than a daily budget.

153
00:15:55,900 --> 00:16:02,900
So, that's why some of you guys will set a budget of $200 per day, and sometimes it spends more than that, as you can see here, it says it may spend up to 350.

154
00:16:02,900 --> 00:16:11,200
And that's because Meta is now trying to spot the days that are really good for performance and just have more wiggle room to spend more on those days.

155
00:16:11,200 --> 00:16:17,880
So, make sure you keep an eye on this, because sometimes Meta has had some weird overspending things happening as they've started to roll this out.

156
00:16:17,880 --> 00:16:26,840
But just so you are clear on what this actually means, Meta will not always spend $200 per day, but by the end of the week, it will likely stay within the $1,400 budget.

157
00:16:27,060 --> 00:16:33,480
Now, when it comes to campaign bid strategy, I know this is another massive topic of bid caps or cost per results or ROAS goals,

158
00:16:33,480 --> 00:16:39,420
and I'll be 100% honest with you, we are simply just running highest volume or value.

159
00:16:39,420 --> 00:16:47,660
Now, this is actually something new that Meta came out with, where it used to be go for highest volume or go for highest value, and they've kind of merged the two together.

160
00:16:47,660 --> 00:16:51,740
But the most important thing here is you need to understand what this is telling the machine.

161
00:16:51,740 --> 00:16:59,880
This is telling the machine that we want it to go and get the highest volume of purchases or the highest value of purchases every single day.

162
00:17:00,120 --> 00:17:06,100
Now, there are a lot of bid cap and cost cap warriors that love to talk about their cost caps and love to talk about their bid caps,

163
00:17:06,360 --> 00:17:09,940
and you know what? I will give them the ability to go and rave about them.

164
00:17:10,040 --> 00:17:15,100
I don't really care about the people that run bid caps or cost caps. They can do that if they please.

165
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:22,640
The biggest thing that I want to clarify is that cost caps and bid caps are not going to save your bad ads.

166
00:17:23,040 --> 00:17:29,260
Again, media buying is simply supporting you. It's not driving the performance; it's supporting the performance.

167
00:17:29,660 --> 00:17:36,680
So, so many people think it's because their cost cap wasn't set properly or their bid cap wasn't set properly, and that's why they haven't been able to scale.

168
00:17:36,980 --> 00:17:42,040
No, it's just because your ads aren't that good or your offer isn't that good. That's why they're not scaling.

