okay, let me show you an actual example of this.

672
01:04:48,180 --> 01:04:53,080
So for example here, you can see batch number 10 was launched May 7th, okay?

673
01:04:53,260 --> 01:04:57,300
And on May 7th, it ran basically for 7 days, okay?

674
01:04:57,400 --> 01:05:02,140
And it spent a total of $8, it got two clicks, and zero purchases.

675
01:05:02,480 --> 01:05:04,580
So this is a great example of a bad ad.

676
01:05:04,800 --> 01:05:08,140
And so what did we do? We simply turned it off because it didn't get spend,

677
01:05:08,140 --> 01:05:11,780
it didn't perform, and there's no reason for us to leave it on.

678
01:05:12,000 --> 01:05:17,960
And so what my point is, is when you're testing this way, if you launch a batch of creatives and they do not get spend

679
01:05:17,960 --> 01:05:21,640
for over 7 days, and we definitely recommend testing for at least 7 days,

680
01:05:21,640 --> 01:05:23,760
if they do not get spend, just turn them off, okay?

681
01:05:23,960 --> 01:05:29,700
If you are one of those people that's like, "Oh, well, sometimes, you know, it takes a while." People say this in Evolve all the time.

682
01:05:29,800 --> 01:05:33,860
"It takes way too long for ad set to pick up. I've seen ads take off after 14 days."

683
01:05:34,040 --> 01:05:35,680
Then leave them on for 14 days, that's fine.

684
01:05:35,680 --> 01:05:38,000
We also have people that just leave ad sets on forever,

685
01:05:38,340 --> 01:05:43,460
but Facebook will actually just automatically start turning them off themselves. It's like a thing that they have in their AI.

686
01:05:43,980 --> 01:05:50,600
But just know what we do is we test for 7 days. And if it's not getting spend and it's not hitting KPI, then we'll just turn it off, okay?

687
01:05:50,600 --> 01:05:54,340
All right. So as you can see on screen, we're now looking at another account here where,

688
01:05:54,340 --> 01:06:00,240
say for example, you have an ad that starts sucking up a bunch of spend and it's missing your KPI.

689
01:06:00,240 --> 01:06:05,720
Well, the easiest thing to do is first thing, go to frequency and check the frequency on the ad.

690
01:06:05,960 --> 01:06:10,720
And for example, if you see an ad that has a higher frequency, like this one here, it has a 2.38,

691
01:06:10,800 --> 01:06:16,320
it's likely going to have a higher return on ad spend just because it is doing more retargeting.

692
01:06:16,440 --> 01:06:20,300
And so the first thing to look at is frequency. Is this doing more retargeting? Yes or no?

693
01:06:20,400 --> 01:06:24,900
And then the second thing to look at, very simple, is how much spend and how much scale is it at?

694
01:06:25,100 --> 01:06:26,880
And the question that you need to ask yourself is,

695
01:06:26,880 --> 01:06:29,620
"Okay, well, we can see these both have pretty low frequencies,

696
01:06:29,620 --> 01:06:32,800
and we can see that there's $7,400 being spent on this,

697
01:06:33,000 --> 01:06:39,280
$6,300 being spent on this, but the ROAS over here is a 1.78, and then the ROAS up here is a 1.38.

698
01:06:39,520 --> 01:06:41,820
Well, if we were to turn off this ad,

699
01:06:42,060 --> 01:06:49,560
we would see that there's about $7,400 more worth of budget that will need to get spread out across all these ads.

700
01:06:49,780 --> 01:06:56,460
And so the question is, if we were to turn off this ad and this spend was to go more into this ad and more into this ad,

701
01:06:56,460 --> 01:07:00,620
do we think that we would have a better overall performance?

702
01:07:00,940 --> 01:07:04,280
And if the answer to that is yes, then you can turn it off.

703
01:07:04,280 --> 01:07:10,820
But for most of the time what you'll notice is that, "Okay, well, if we were to basically double the budget on this ad,

704
01:07:10,820 --> 01:07:16,880
so add another $7,000 on top of this ad, would we be able to keep a 1.5? Probably not, right?

705
01:07:17,040 --> 01:07:21,880
And then down here, if we were to add another $7,000 on top of this ad, so again double the budget,

706
01:07:22,060 --> 01:07:25,440
would it be kept at a 1.78? Probably not.

707
01:07:25,620 --> 01:07:30,100
And so the main thing that you have to look at is just these theoreticals more or less

708
01:07:30,100 --> 01:07:34,960
of, okay, this is a judgment call. I am confident that, you know, we have ads that are strong enough,

709
01:07:34,960 --> 01:07:38,840
like this one down here is at a, you know, 1.8 and it's not really getting a lot of spend,

710
01:07:38,840 --> 01:07:40,780
so I think it could get some more spend and etc.

711
01:07:41,120 --> 01:07:46,020
Then if you feel confident that this spend can be divided across the account, then go ahead and turn the ad off.

712
01:07:46,240 --> 01:07:47,860
A lot of times it's not always obvious.

713
01:07:47,860 --> 01:07:53,120
But when you have a new ad that starts sucking up a bunch of spend right away and it's really missing KPI,

714
01:07:53,120 --> 01:07:56,120
the first thing that you need to check is how is the overall ROAS?

715
01:07:56,420 --> 01:08:00,860
And then from there, if I were to turn this ad off, would all of these other ads be better?

716
01:08:00,860 --> 01:08:07,640
And just know that if you do have ads that are getting a lot of spend but are missing KPI, it's mostly because they are misleading.

717
01:08:07,640 --> 01:08:11,960
And again, if you do see this happening a lot, what you can actually do is you can set,

718
01:08:11,960 --> 01:08:15,280
again, maximum budgets on your creative testing ad sets.

719
01:08:15,280 --> 01:08:20,240
But, again, we don't usually do this because as long as you're not making misleading ads, you're going to be okay.

720
01:08:20,240 --> 01:08:23,380
Right. So those are the two scenarios that happen from this,

721
01:08:23,580 --> 01:08:26,960
and with that said, that is everything inside of this campaign.

722
01:08:27,020 --> 01:08:31,500
Now, if you're still hanging in there with me, we got a few more things to cover, and then we're going to wrap this thing up.

723
01:08:31,540 --> 01:08:37,340
But the next thing on here that we will actually run is something called a raw content campaign.

724
01:08:37,380 --> 01:08:40,920
And so what we do with a raw content campaign is quite simple.

725
01:08:40,920 --> 01:08:45,440
This is mainly for our brands that are now doing above $1,000 or $2,000 per day,

726
01:08:45,440 --> 01:08:50,140
and what we will call this is our raw content campaign, and this is just again another US CBO.

727
01:08:50,560 --> 01:08:58,980
And then inside of here we are now going to actually launch specifically creative batches only that are just are mass creative volume testing.

728
01:08:59,360 --> 01:09:08,840
And so inside of here we don't actually do the two landing page tests. We actually only just do one landing page because we are just basically sending it on ads.

729
01:09:08,960 --> 01:09:14,740
And so these are just what we would call raw content batches, raw content, or sometimes we call them UGC.

730
01:09:15,200 --> 01:09:22,920
So just any kind of product seeding videos that we get back from creators or any type of AI stuff, just stuff that you're not putting a lot of intent into,

731
01:09:23,180 --> 01:09:26,680
we will load these in batches again of 3 to 5.

732
01:09:26,900 --> 01:09:36,200
Sometimes we'll cut them, meaning that if we get a video that's 90 seconds, we will add some different starting points, or even if we get a video that's 30 seconds, sometimes we add different starting points.

733
01:09:36,460 --> 01:09:41,700
But for the most part, this raw content campaign is more for brands that just have tons of volume that they want to test.

734
01:09:41,900 --> 01:09:47,560
And inside of this campaign we don't really abide by this rule. We just kind of send as many creatives in here as possible,

735
01:09:47,560 --> 01:09:49,220
and we just see what hits.

736
01:09:49,220 --> 01:09:53,380
And so this is just where we kind of unload things, and we're not really putting a lot of attention.

737
01:09:53,380 --> 01:09:56,900
It's just creative that we got that we just want to throw in the account and test.

738
01:09:56,900 --> 01:10:02,720
And again, inside of here we don't run minimum budgets, especially on this, because we're not putting a lot of intent into this.

739
01:10:02,720 --> 01:10:11,480
But the only other thing that I want to mention is, again, if you're doing less than $1,000 a day in budget, you don't need to really have a raw content campaign, because you probably don't have that much content coming in.

740
01:10:11,480 --> 01:10:16,740
But if you do have a lot of content for whatever reason, you can also launch a raw content campaign.

741
01:10:16,740 --> 01:10:22,900
And again, this is just great for basically spam testing and just throwing a bunch of ad sets in there and hoping something actually sticks.

742
01:10:23,200 --> 01:10:28,180
And now the only other type of campaign that we will run is going to be a whitelisting campaign.

743
01:10:28,240 --> 01:10:36,120
So, same thing here. What we're actually going to do, and to make it simple, I'm just going to duplicate the acquisition campaign, so we're going to duplicate this,

744
01:10:36,120 --> 01:10:38,820
and this will actually just be a whitelisting campaign.

745
01:10:38,820 --> 01:10:47,700
And what this whitelisting campaign is, is basically just an ad set where we will set up our ads, but change the profile to the actual creator.

746
01:10:47,700 --> 01:10:56,400
So, for example, when you are setting up ads, and again, this is in the whitelisting campaign, so I'm just going to delete this ad set here and show you how we would actually set this up.

747
01:10:56,400 --> 01:11:02,200
So again, for the whitelisting ads, rather than using our own page, we would simply use the creator's page.

748
01:11:02,200 --> 01:11:08,340
And if you don't know what this is, you're like, "What the heck is that?", I actually have another video coming out on whitelisting.

749
01:11:08,740 --> 01:11:13,820
We currently have all of those whitelisting modules inside of the Evolve program, but I will be making a new video on it.

750
01:11:13,820 --> 01:11:19,920
And there honestly is a lot of value in running whitelisting, specifically because it looks like it's coming from a real person.

751
01:11:19,920 --> 01:11:27,900
And so, again, if you don't know what this looks like, this is an example of what a whitelisting ad would look like, where again, we have the same UGC creative.

752
01:11:27,900 --> 01:11:30,860
So if I go ahead and just upload a image here,

753
01:11:30,860 --> 01:11:36,660
you can see that rather than it saying Easy A Media, it actually says this creator's name, which is Chryssa, who is awesome,

754
01:11:36,860 --> 01:11:39,240
and you can see it comes from her profile now.

755
01:11:39,400 --> 01:11:48,160
And so whitelisting is again a really great way to kind of camouflage in the feed and make it look very, very organic with the UGC videos that you are testing.

756
01:11:48,160 --> 01:11:54,720
And so just so you have context, whenever we find a new UGC video that does really, really well in our raw content campaign,

757
01:11:54,760 --> 01:12:00,820
because this is where we mainly test most of our product seeding or, you know, UGC content that we generate with our UGC team,

758
01:12:01,000 --> 01:12:04,040
we will then, if it becomes a winning ad that gets spend and hits KPI,

759
01:12:04,400 --> 01:12:10,960
we will then reach out to the creator, we will get whitelisting access, which again, I'll have a video coming on it soon. It's just a whole lot to cover.

760
01:12:11,100 --> 01:12:14,220
But inside of this whitelisting campaign, we will have our whitelisting ad.

761
01:12:14,260 --> 01:12:21,140
I mean, Facebook is just glitching out. It would just be this Champions Whitelisting ad set with the whitelisted ad in there,

762
01:12:21,200 --> 01:12:24,460
and again, this would be the creator's UGC video on their profile.

763
01:12:24,780 --> 01:12:29,360
And so, those are the four types of campaigns that we are currently running.

764
01:12:29,360 --> 01:12:32,740
There's honestly nothing more that we test other than this.

765
01:12:33,000 --> 01:12:41,940
Now, the first thing that I want you to understand is that when it comes to testing different countries, you need to make sure that you just start with a main acquisition campaign, okay?

766
01:12:41,980 --> 01:12:46,880
You don't need to add all of these other campaigns until you actually find winners in those countries.

767
01:12:47,220 --> 01:12:56,200
Most people, we recommend running top five, and so the top five countries are US, Canada, UK, Australia, and New Zealand. Those are the top five.

768
01:12:56,400 --> 01:13:01,740
With that said, in most of our brands, what we will actually do is we will have one campaign for US.

769
01:13:01,760 --> 01:13:10,240
The second campaign that we'll have will be CA, AU, and UK. And what that stands for is Canada, Australia, and UK.

770
01:13:10,580 --> 01:13:15,600
And so, we will have an acquisition campaign for Canada, Australia, and UK.

771
01:13:16,020 --> 01:13:29,200
And then we would also have another campaign, which would be for Europe, specifically, as we have found some success with Europe for a few of our brands in countries like Germany and Switzerland, and all that kind of stuff. We love Europe.

772
01:13:29,460 --> 01:13:33,300
And so inside of here, we will actually have multiple acquisition campaigns.

773
01:13:33,300 --> 01:13:40,200
And this is where it's very important, because what we actually do here is we test creatives inside of this campaign as well.

774
01:13:40,360 --> 01:13:49,920
And if I refresh here, you'll see that the creative batch number one ad set is inside of this campaign, because we actually test each and every single creative in each country as well.

775
01:13:49,920 --> 01:13:58,360
And the reason for that is, certain creatives that perform well in the US, sometimes they don't actually perform well in Canada, or Australia, or UK, or in Europe.

776
01:13:58,580 --> 01:14:03,840
And so, it's very, very important that we test individual creatives inside of each country as well.

777
01:14:04,040 --> 01:14:16,740
And now, one thing that's super important is that if you are testing multiple countries, that you do use the Breakdown Tool to be able to see the country that you are sending to and the country that is performing best, especially if you are bundling them together.

778
01:14:16,760 --> 01:14:22,140
Now, we do have brands that evolve as well, that will just run a strict worldwide campaign.

779
01:14:22,140 --> 01:14:29,060
And so, inside of here to set up a worldwide campaign, basically, what you would do is you would scroll down into the location section,

780
01:14:29,060 --> 01:14:34,540
and you would literally just type in "worldwide," and you would select every single country.

781
01:14:34,540 --> 01:14:37,400
Now, when you do this, you definitely are sending it a little bit.

782
01:14:37,400 --> 01:14:46,340
And what we would recommend is that if you have a US campaign running, you can just exclude the United States. So, that way it's just worldwide, excluding the United States.

783
01:14:46,340 --> 01:14:49,320
Now, some people still just overlap, put the US in there as well.

784
01:14:49,320 --> 01:14:56,400
But again, this would be a worldwide CBO. And if this is easier for you, and you just want to keep things more consolidated, you can do that as well.

785
01:14:56,580 --> 01:14:58,620
But just for the sake of simplicity, I'm going to remove this here.

786
01:14:58,820 --> 01:15:04,780
But for the sake of this video, again, what we would recommend doing is just starting with the top five first,

787
01:15:04,780 --> 01:15:12,060
because what can happen when you start running worldwide is you can start to see a lot of really bad countries picking up spend and not really doing anything.

788
01:15:12,060 --> 01:15:24,260
So, it is important that if you are doing that, again, you go to breakdown, you go to country, and you look at which countries are getting spend but not getting performance, and then you can start to exclude those from your country list.

789
01:15:24,500 --> 01:15:29,900
So, again, you would go down into the exclusion side of things, and you would start to exclude a bunch of different countries.

790
01:15:30,140 --> 01:15:33,040
Now, there are some bulk exclusions that you can add as well.

791
01:15:33,040 --> 01:15:36,720
But at the end of the day, this is how you can test worldwide if you do want to test worldwide.

792
01:15:37,160 --> 01:15:44,140
The most important thing is it's just, keep in mind, we test creatives and landing pages and offers in each and every single acquisition campaign.

793
01:15:44,180 --> 01:15:46,840
So, that is all of the account structures.

794
01:15:46,840 --> 01:15:54,400
And as you get to more scale, and say you want to launch ASCs for your Canadian, AU, and UK countries as well, you can do that.

795
01:15:54,580 --> 01:16:00,980
And if you want to launch raw content, you can as well. And if you want to launch whitelisting, you can. And that's when campaigns start to get a little bit more complex.

796
01:16:00,980 --> 01:16:09,220
There are also strategies where brands will, as you get really big, start to move into different ad accounts. So, you'll have a US ad account or a Canadian ad account.

797
01:16:09,460 --> 01:16:14,440
There is no right or wrong with that. It's really just preference of how brands choose to set things up.

798
01:16:14,440 --> 01:16:22,520
But the brands at the biggest level that we work with usually have dedicated accounts for the US, or dedicated accounts for Australia, or Canada, or wherever their big markets are.

799
01:16:22,660 --> 01:16:26,300
But just know, if you are not at that stage, you don't have to get overwhelmed by this.

800
01:16:26,300 --> 01:16:29,720
If we just filter by the things that matter, you should honestly only have two campaigns.

801
01:16:29,880 --> 01:16:36,480
And then as you start to get more sophisticated, maybe you'll add in a raw content campaign, where now you have three, total.

802
01:16:36,560 --> 01:16:41,360
And then if you have winning ads coming from the raw content or from your creators, then you will have a whitelisting campaign.

803
01:16:41,360 --> 01:16:52,980
And then as you expand further, potentially, you have Canada, or UK, or worldwide running, and then, again, you can duplicate those out as well for raw content, ASC, and whatever. I'm not going to do that for the sake of this video, because that's a little bit more advanced.

804
01:16:53,120 --> 01:16:59,180
But that is everything that we do inside of the ad account, that's how we structure things, and that is how we creative test.

805
01:16:59,380 --> 01:17:02,360
Now, when it comes to the scaling side of things, it's pretty simple.

806
01:17:02,360 --> 01:17:06,860
I'm just going to filter by selection to clean up the ad account a little bit here.

807
01:17:06,860 --> 01:17:12,540
So, for scaling, all we're looking for is 48 to 72 hours of above KPI performance.

808
01:17:12,540 --> 01:17:22,860
And so, for example, if our KPI is a 2x ROAS and we can see that we're getting a 2x ROAS and it's been like that for 48 to 72 hours, well, you can bump by 20%.

809
01:17:23,180 --> 01:17:27,520
And a 20% bump would obviously be $40. So, you could scale that to 240.

810
01:17:27,520 --> 01:17:32,900
Now, say, your target KPI is a 2x ROAS, and you're getting a 3x or 3.5.

811
01:17:32,900 --> 01:17:40,900
You can actually do a more aggressive bump of 50%. So, scaling from 200 to 300 would be a 50% bump, again, because 50%.

812
01:17:41,340 --> 01:17:48,980
And if you need simple math, this is what I always do, I just use the calculator and I times by this. So, I'll do 200 times 1.5, that's 300, right?

813
01:17:48,980 --> 01:17:53,360
Because we want to times by 1, because we want to keep our budget, and then we want to add 50%, right?

814
01:17:53,360 --> 01:17:57,100
And then if you want to do 20%, it would just be times 1.2, so, 240, right?

815
01:17:57,100 --> 01:18:05,080
And if you are way above KPI, so, say, for example, you're spending $300 per day, and your target's a 2, and you're getting a 5x, you can actually do some more aggressive

816
01:18:05,080 --> 01:18:12,980
campaign bumps, like doubling, but just keep in mind that when you double and as you get to more scale, like for example, we have brands spending $6,000 a day.

817
01:18:13,120 --> 01:18:20,340
So it's very rare that we'll double because obviously a 20% bump here is a pretty significant amount, let alone a double to 12K would be a bit of a send.

818
01:18:20,580 --> 01:18:25,980
But the point is, is we usually scale by 20, 50, or 100%, depending on where you're at.

819
01:18:26,100 --> 01:18:34,480
Just know that the smaller your budget, usually the more aggressive you can be. Just be prepared that you might disrupt your performance if you scale by more than 50 or 100%.

820
01:18:34,480 --> 01:18:41,020
But usually the reason why things do not scale if you are someone that has scaled and nothing has changed in the account,

821
01:18:41,160 --> 01:18:49,240
so say you've had these ads running for, you know, months and then all of a sudden you just start to see two good days of performance, just know that there hasn't been any reason

822
01:18:49,240 --> 01:18:55,540
for why performance should get better, other than the market. Whereas, for example, if we launch creative batch number one,

823
01:18:55,540 --> 01:19:01,340
and this thing starts sucking up spend and the ROAS goes from like a two overall to like a 5x,

824
01:19:01,340 --> 01:19:04,040
and we can tell it's clearly because of this ad,

825
01:19:04,260 --> 01:19:13,400
well, in that instance we're a lot more confident to scale. So sometimes we'll bump by 50 or 100% because we know like, holy cow, we just hit a crazy winning ad

826
01:19:13,400 --> 01:19:19,500
and that is the reason why performance is better and not just because it's a good week or it's been a couple of good days. So keep that mind in scaling.

827
01:19:19,980 --> 01:19:28,480
If you do have a reason of why performance has been better, like you got a new offer, a new creative, or a new landing page, then it makes sense to be more aggressive with your scaling.

828
01:19:28,780 --> 01:19:34,680
But if it's just been the same ads running and you just magically have better performance, I'd be a little bit more weary about scaling there.

829
01:19:34,680 --> 01:19:40,820
And that's what happens to a lot of people is they're just scaling based off of the market and not based on any new creatives or anything like that.

830
01:19:41,080 --> 01:19:50,200
And so that is how we scale. And now for the final part of this video, I'm just going to answer some really common FAQs that we get to hopefully address them before they get asked in the comments down below.

831
01:19:50,460 --> 01:19:59,820
And then we'll be able to wrap this video up. All right, so as you can see here, we're actually inside of Evolve and we're going to look at some of these FAQs, which is the how many creative ad sets can you launch?

832
01:19:59,920 --> 01:20:06,620
Um again, this is what we say just rule of thumb is five ad sets, but this is actually the new rule that we have, so just keep that in mind.

833
01:20:06,620 --> 01:20:11,000
Do we set min and max spends? We obviously went over that. How long do we test creators for? We went over that as well.

834
01:20:11,000 --> 01:20:13,800
How do you know when to leave a winner on? We did cover that as well.

835
01:20:13,800 --> 01:20:21,420
Now, the only other question that I'm going to answer because I know it's probably going to get asked below is, do you test images and videos in the same campaign? Yes, we do.

836
01:20:21,540 --> 01:20:25,660
And a lot of the times images are actually our top-spending ads, even for brands that are at scale.

837
01:20:25,940 --> 01:20:30,680
The reason why your images are not getting spend compared to videos is because the images are just not that good.

838
01:20:30,680 --> 01:20:35,780
And it's actually a lot harder to make a really good image ad that converts than it is to make a video.

839
01:20:35,920 --> 01:20:43,340
And again, they're both hard, but the point is, is we test both in the same campaign. We don't segment images and videos. We just test them together, okay?

